-- Q12 [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · medium · Skill 2 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans C] (g59)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions',
    $qt59$When aqueous hydrochloric acid is added to aqueous sodium carbonate, the molecular equation is \(2\,\text{HCl}(aq) + \text{Na}_2\text{CO}_3(aq) \rightarrow 2\,\text{NaCl}(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) + \text{CO}_2(g)\). After identifying and removing the spectator ions, which equation is the correctly balanced net ionic equation for this reaction?$qt59$,
    $op59$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(2\\,\\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq) + \\text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g) + 2\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(2\\,\\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(2\\,\\text{Na}^+(aq) + \\text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{O}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\)"}]$op59$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex59$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the net ionic equation is found by writing every strong electrolyte as its dissociated ions, then cancelling the ions that appear unchanged on both sides. The complete ionic equation is \(2\,\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\,\text{Cl}^-(aq) + 2\,\text{Na}^+(aq) + \text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\,\text{Na}^+(aq) + 2\,\text{Cl}^-(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) + \text{CO}_2(g)\). The water and carbon dioxide are molecular products and stay intact, while the \(2\,\text{Na}^+\) and \(2\,\text{Cl}^-\) ions appear identically on both sides and are therefore the spectator ions; eliminating them leaves \(2\,\text{H}^+(aq) + \text{CO}_3^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) + \text{CO}_2(g)\). This equation is balanced in both atoms (H: \(2 = 2\); C: \(1 = 1\); O: \(3 = 1 + 2 = 3\)) and charge (left \(= 2(+1) + (-2) = 0\); right \(= 0\)), confirming A. (Choice A) This retains \(\text{Cl}^-\) on both sides, but an ion that appears unchanged on each side is by definition a spectator and must be cancelled, so a true net ionic equation cannot contain \(2\,\text{Cl}^-\) on the left and right. (Choice B) This drops the coefficient on \(\text{H}^+\) to 1, which unbalances both hydrogen (1 on the left versus 2 in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\)) and charge (left \(= (+1) + (-2) = -1\) versus 0 on the right); two protons are required to consume one carbonate. (Choice D) This keeps \(\text{Na}^+\) as a participant rather than recognizing it as a spectator and invents a solid \(\text{Na}_2\text{O}\) product; in aqueous acid the carbonate is protonated by \(\text{H}^+\) to give water and \(\text{CO}_2\), and sodium remains a freely dissolved spectator ion. Skill 2$ex59$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc59$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc59$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a59A$failed to cancel chloride spectator ion$a59A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a59B$dropped H+ coefficient leaving equation unbalanced$a59B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a59D$treated sodium as a reactant and invented Na2O product$a59D$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · medium · Skill 3 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans D] (g60)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions',
    $qt60$When aqueous lead(II) nitrate is combined with aqueous sodium chloride, a white precipitate forms according to the balanced molecular equation \(\text{Pb(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\,\text{NaCl}(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbCl}_2(s) + 2\,\text{NaNO}_3(aq)\). Treating every soluble salt as a fully dissociated strong electrolyte, which choice correctly identifies the spectator ions and the balanced net ionic equation for this reaction?$qt60$,
    $op60$[{"label":"A","text":"No reaction occurs, so all four ions, \\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}\\), \\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\), \\(\\text{Na}^+\\), and \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\), are spectator ions and no net ionic equation can be written."},{"label":"B","text":"The spectator ions are \\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}\\) and \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\), and the net ionic equation is \\(\\text{Na}^+(aq) + \\text{NO}_3^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{NaNO}_3(s)\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"The spectator ions are \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) and \\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\), and the net ionic equation is \\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq) + \\text{Cl}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{PbCl}(s)\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"The spectator ions are \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) and \\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\), and the net ionic equation is \\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq) + 2\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{PbCl}_2(s)\\)."}]$op60$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex60$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the only species that changes state across the reaction are the lead and chloride ions, which combine to form the insoluble solid \(\text{PbCl}_2(s)\). Writing every soluble strong electrolyte as separated aqueous ions gives the complete ionic equation \(\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{NO}_3^-(aq) + 2\,\text{Na}^+(aq) + 2\,\text{Cl}^-(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbCl}_2(s) + 2\,\text{Na}^+(aq) + 2\,\text{NO}_3^-(aq)\). The \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{NO}_3^-\) ions appear unchanged on both sides, so they are the spectator ions and are canceled, leaving the net ionic equation \(\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{Cl}^-(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbCl}_2(s)\). The coefficient of \(2\) on \(\text{Cl}^-\) is required because lead carries a \(+2\) charge and each chloride a \(-1\) charge, so two chlorides are needed to give the neutral \(\text{PbCl}_2\) formula and to balance both mass and charge. (Choice A) A reaction does occur because \(\text{PbCl}_2\) is insoluble and precipitates out of solution, so \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\) are removed from solution and are not spectators. (Choice B) This reverses the roles of the ions: \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\) are the ions that actually combine into the precipitate, so they cannot be spectators, and \(\text{NaNO}_3\) is freely soluble and does not precipitate, so the proposed net ionic equation does not occur. (Choice C) The spectators are correctly identified, but the net ionic equation uses an incorrect \(1:1\) ratio and the nonexistent formula \(\text{PbCl}\); lead(II) requires two chloride ions to balance its \(+2\) charge, giving \(\text{PbCl}_2\). Skill 3$ex60$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc60$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc60$, 'general-chemistry', 3, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a60A$Assuming a double-displacement mixture produces no reaction and treating all ions as spectators$a60A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a60B$Swapping the ions that form the precipitate with the truly spectator ions$a60B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a60C$Using a 1:1 ion ratio that ignores the +2 charge on lead, yielding the nonexistent formula PbCl$a60C$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · hard · Skill 4 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans A] (g61)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions',
    $qt61$Acetic acid is titrated with sodium hydroxide according to the balanced molecular equation \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq) + \text{NaOH}(aq) \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{COONa}(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\). When this reaction is rewritten as a net ionic equation, which reactant species must be written in its undissociated molecular form?$qt61$,
    $op61$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}\\), because it is a weak acid that exists predominantly as intact molecules in solution"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{NaOH}\\), because it is a base and bases are not separated into ions in net ionic equations"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COONa}\\), because the dissolved salt remains as neutral formula units in solution"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Na}^+\\), because it is the spectator ion and spectator species are kept undissociated"}]$op61$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex61$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the rule for writing net ionic equations is that only strong electrolytes that are fully dissociated in solution are split into ions, while weak electrolytes, gases, and insoluble solids are written as intact units. Acetic acid, \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\), is a weak acid (a weak electrolyte): only a small fraction ionizes, so it exists predominantly as undissociated molecules and is written as \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq)\). In contrast, \(\text{NaOH}\) is a strong base that dissociates completely to \(\text{Na}^+ + \text{OH}^-\), and the soluble salt \(\text{CH}_3\text{COONa}\) dissociates completely to \(\text{Na}^+ + \text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\); both are split into ions. Liquid water is a molecular product. Identifying \(\text{Na}^+\) as the spectator (it appears unchanged on both sides) and cancelling it yields the net ionic equation \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq) + \text{OH}^-(aq) \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\), in which the only reactant kept as a molecule is the weak acid. (Choice B) \(\text{NaOH}\) is a strong base and a strong electrolyte; it dissociates fully into \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{OH}^-\), so it is the \(\text{OH}^-\) ion—not an intact molecule—that appears as a reactant, and the blanket claim that bases are never separated is false. (Choice C) \(\text{CH}_3\text{COONa}\) is a soluble ionic salt and strong electrolyte that dissociates completely into \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\); it is also a product, not a reactant, so it cannot be the reactant written in molecular form. (Choice D) \(\text{Na}^+\) is indeed the spectator ion, but spectator ions are written in their dissociated ionic form and then cancelled from both sides; being a spectator does not mean a species is kept undissociated as a molecule. Skill 4$ex61$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc61$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc61$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
