-- Q15 [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · hard · Skill 4 · 4E Stoichiometry (GC) · ans B] (g62)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Oxidation-Reduction Reactions', 'Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions',
    $qt62$When aqueous calcium chloride is mixed with aqueous sodium phosphate, a precipitate forms according to the balanced molecular equation \(3\,\text{CaCl}_2(aq) + 2\,\text{Na}_3\text{PO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s) + 6\,\text{NaCl}(aq)\). Using the solubility rules and treating every soluble salt as a fully dissociated strong electrolyte, which choice correctly represents the net ionic equation for this reaction?$qt62$,
    $op62$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + \\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{CaPO}_4(s)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 2\\,\\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca}_3(\\text{PO}_4)_2(s)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 6\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca}_3\\text{Cl}_6(s)\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 6\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq) + 6\\,\\text{Na}^+(aq) + 2\\,\\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca}_3(\\text{PO}_4)_2(s) + 6\\,\\text{Na}^+(aq) + 6\\,\\text{Cl}^-(aq)\\)"}]$op62$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex62$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because writing the net ionic equation requires three steps: dissociate every soluble strong electrolyte, identify the spectator ions, and cancel them. Dissociating the reactants gives \(3\,\text{CaCl}_2 \rightarrow 3\,\text{Ca}^{2+} + 6\,\text{Cl}^-\) and \(2\,\text{Na}_3\text{PO}_4 \rightarrow 6\,\text{Na}^+ + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-}\); the products are the insoluble precipitate \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s)\), which stays written as an intact solid, plus \(6\,\text{NaCl} \rightarrow 6\,\text{Na}^+ + 6\,\text{Cl}^-\). The complete ionic equation is \(3\,\text{Ca}^{2+} + 6\,\text{Cl}^- + 6\,\text{Na}^+ + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-} \rightarrow \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s) + 6\,\text{Na}^+ + 6\,\text{Cl}^-\). The \(6\,\text{Na}^+\) and \(6\,\text{Cl}^-\) appear unchanged on both sides, so they are the only spectator ions and cancel, leaving \(3\,\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s)\). Charge is conserved: the left side carries \(3(+2) + 2(-3) = +6 - 6 = 0\), matching the neutral solid on the right, and the 3:2 cation-to-anion ratio reproduces the formula \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\). (Choice A) This reduces the coefficients to a 1:1 ratio and invents the formula \(\text{CaPO}_4\), which is not charge-balanced (\(+2\) plus \(-3\) gives \(-1\), not zero) and contradicts the \(3:2\) stoichiometry fixed by the \(2+\) and \(3-\) ion charges; the precipitate must be \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\). (Choice C) This keeps the wrong pair of ions and precipitates a chloride solid, but \(\text{Cl}^-\) is a spectator here because \(\text{CaCl}_2\) and \(\text{NaCl}\) are both soluble; no \(\text{Ca}_3\text{Cl}_6\) species exists, and the actual precipitate is the phosphate, so the reacting ions are \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and \(\text{PO}_4^{3-}\), not \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Cl}^-\). (Choice D) This is the complete ionic equation written before cancellation; it correctly shows all dissociated species but fails the final step of removing the \(6\,\text{Na}^+\) and \(6\,\text{Cl}^-\) spectator ions that appear identically on both sides, so it is not yet the net ionic equation. Skill 4$ex62$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc62$Stoichiometry (GC)$cc62$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
